<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tajlu Khanum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1540">1540</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Tajli Begum, Shah-Begi Khanum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tajlu Khanum, also known by her title Shah-Begi Khanum, was a Turkoman princess of the Mawsillu tribe of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> confederation who became the principal wife of Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a> and the mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahmasp-i">Tahmasp I</a>. Married to Ismail in 1503 or 1504, she was his most favoured wife and an adviser to whom nobles in disgrace turned for help. When Ismail died in 1524 she helped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a> leaders enthrone the ten-year-old Tahmasp, became queen mother and held power behind the throne for at least eight years, even sending an envoy to the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. Suspected of favouring her son Bahram Mirza, she was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>, where she died in 1540.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tajlu-khanum.jpg" alt="Tajlu Khanum" width="330" height="418" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 16th century artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Likely_depiction_of_Tajlu_Khanum,_Shah_Tahmasp's_mother._The_Meeting_of_Kay-Qobad_with_his_Brother_Kay-Kavus,_painted_circa_1531.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Tajlu Khanum</b>, Ismail I’s queen, financed the Golden Iwan of the Fatima Masumeh Shrine at Qom in 1519, built her husband’s tomb at Ardabil and supported Tahmasp’s accession in 1524.</blockquote><a href="/societies/safavid-iran">Social Hierarchy of Safavid Iran</a></li></ul></section>
